Q3 – Resource Safety, Debugging and Robust Code (7 Marks)
This question focuses on Java file handling, resource leaks, debugging, try-catch-finally and try-with-resources.

We need to create a daily sales report generator that reads multiple store files and writes their total sales into one summary file.

I'll provide both implementations, expected output, Student A and Student B answers, resource-closing explanations, and complexity analysis.
1. Shared Group Work – Manual Resource Management
A. Java program using try-catch-finally
In this approach, we open files manually and close them using close() inside the finally block.

import java.io.*;

public class SalesReportManual {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        BufferedReader reader = null;
        BufferedWriter writer = null;

        String[] files = {
            "store1.txt",
            "store2.txt",
            "store3.txt"
        };

        try {
            writer = new BufferedWriter(
                new FileWriter("sales_report.txt")
            );

            for (String file : files) {

                reader = new BufferedReader(
                    new FileReader(file)
                );

                String line;
                int total = 0;

                while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                    total += Integer.parseInt(line);
                }

                writer.write(file + " Total: " + total);
                writer.newLine();

                reader.close();
                reader = null;
            }

            System.out.println("Report generated successfully.");

        } catch (IOException | NumberFormatException e) {

            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());

        } finally {

            try {
                if (reader != null)
                    reader.close();

                if (writer != null)
                    writer.close();

            } catch (IOException e) {
                System.out.println("Error closing files: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}



